body:
## Issue Description

### Problem Summary
There is a functional inconsistency between the STM32G0 and STM32H7 series HAL libraries in the [HAL_UART_IRQHandler](file://D:\work\2025FLUX\flux_temp\temp_10_20_485\Drivers\STM32G0xx_HAL_Driver\Src\stm32g0xx_hal_uart.c#L2304-L2627) implementation. Specifically, the STM32G0 version lacks the logic to handle IDLE events in DMA circular mode, which can lead to improper event notification to the application layer under certain usage scenarios.

### Detailed Description

1. **Inconsistent DMA Circular Mode Checking Approach**:
   - STM32G0 uses [HAL_IS_BIT_CLR(huart->hdmarx->Instance->CCR, DMA_CCR_CIRC)](file://D:\work\2025FLUX\flux_temp\temp_10_20_485\Openlib\perf_counter\CI\perf_counter_template_gcc\Drivers\STM32F1xx_HAL_Driver\Inc\stm32f1xx_hal_def.h#L59-L60)
   - STM32H7 uses `huart->hdmarx->Init.Mode != DMA_CIRCULAR`

2. **Missing IDLE Event Handling in Circular Mode**:
   The STM32H7 version includes the following logic in [HAL_UART_IRQHandler](file://D:\work\2025FLUX\flux_temp\temp_10_20_485\Drivers\STM32G0xx_HAL_Driver\Src\stm32g0xx_hal_uart.c#L2304-L2627) that is missing in the STM32G0 version:
   ```c
   /* If DMA is in Circular mode, Idle event is to be reported to user
      even if occurring after a Transfer Complete event from DMA */
   if (nb_remaining_rx_data == huart->RxXferSize)
   {
     if (huart->hdmarx->Init.Mode == DMA_CIRCULAR)
     {
       /* Initialize type of RxEvent that correspond to RxEvent callback execution;
          In this case, Rx Event type is Idle Event */
       huart->RxEventType = HAL_UART_RXEVENT_IDLE;
       // Callback invocation
     }
   }
   ```


### Impact
When using DMA circular mode for UART reception, if an IDLE line event occurs after the reception buffer is filled, the STM32G0 version will not notify the application layer. This may result in delayed data processing or missed event notifications.

### Expected Fix
It is recommended to unify the [HAL_UART_IRQHandler](file://D:\work\2025FLUX\flux_temp\temp_10_20_485\Drivers\STM32G0xx_HAL_Driver\Src\stm32g0xx_hal_uart.c#L2304-L2627) implementation between STM32G0 and STM32H7 series, enhancing the STM32G0 version's functional completeness to ensure consistent behavior across all usage scenarios. This would involve:
1. Standardizing the DMA circular mode detection method
2. Adding the missing IDLE event handling logic for circular mode operation

### Environment
- MCU Series: STM32G0
- HAL Library Version: [Please specify version]
- Affected Module: UART HAL driver ([stm32g0xx_hal_uart.c](file://D:\work\2025FLUX\flux_temp\temp_10_20_485\Drivers\STM32G0xx_HAL_Driver\Src\stm32g0xx_hal_uart.c))
- Function: [HAL_UART_IRQHandler](file://D:\work\2025FLUX\flux_temp\temp_10_20_485\Drivers\STM32G0xx_HAL_Driver\Src\stm32g0xx_hal_uart.c#L2304-L2627)

In STM32G0xx_hal_conf.h, these macros are defined:

#define  VDD_VALUE                    (3300UL)                                        /*!< Value of VDD in mv */
#define  TICK_INT_PRIORITY            0U /*!< tick interrupt priority */
#define  USE_RTOS                     0U
#define  PREFETCH_ENABLE              0U
#define  INSTRUCTION_CACHE_ENABLE     1U

As far as I can tell, PREFETCH_ENABLE is not implemented and has no affect on any of the library code. In order to disable prefetch we had to add the following in our main.c after SystemClock_Config():

  // CRITICAL: Explicitly disable prefetch for STM32G0 errata 2.2.10
  // The PREFETCH_ENABLE define in hal_conf.h is NOT used by the HAL
  // We must manually clear the PRFTEN bit in FLASH->ACR
  // Confirm that prefetch is not disabled by setting PREFETCH_ENABLE to 0
  if (READ_BIT(FLASH->ACR, FLASH_ACR_PRFTEN) != 0) {
    CLEAR_BIT(FLASH->ACR, FLASH_ACR_PRFTEN);
  }

  // Verify prefetch is actually disabled
  if (READ_BIT(FLASH->ACR, FLASH_ACR_PRFTEN) != 0) {
    // Prefetch failed to disable - this should never happen
    Error_Handler();
  }

As noted in my comment above, this directly addresses the "silicon bug" described in errata 2.2.10, as anyone attempting to disable prefetch in the CubeMX/IDE GUI may not realize that doing this in the gui (or the header) has no effect. 

We fought random hardfaults for 2 months to no avail until we discovered this bug, and lost an hour or so not realizing that we had to manually disable prefetch with the above code. 

For what it's worth, I'm not sure INSTRUCTION_CACHE_ENABLE is implemented either.

**Describe the set-up**
Custom board with an STM32G0B0CET6

**Describe the bug**
An incorrect initialization assert prevents the use of DMA when using USART5 peripheral on the STM32G0B0CETx.

**How To Reproduce**
1. Initialize the USART5 peripheral to use DMA for TX. Start the program.

2. The cause of the problem is an assert failed during `assert_param(IS_DMA_ALL_REQUEST(hdma->Init.Request));` in stm32g0xx_hal_dma.c at line 170.

The initialization code to reproduce the problem:
```    // Configure DMA for USART5 TX
    hdma_usart5_tx.Instance = USART5_TX_DMA_Channel;
    hdma_usart5_tx.Init.Request = DMA_REQUEST_USART5_TX;
    hdma_usart5_tx.Init.Direction = DMA_MEMORY_TO_PERIPH;
    hdma_usart5_tx.Init.PeriphInc = DMA_PINC_DISABLE;
    hdma_usart5_tx.Init.MemInc = DMA_MINC_ENABLE;
    hdma_usart5_tx.Init.PeriphDataAlignment = DMA_PDATAALIGN_BYTE;
    hdma_usart5_tx.Init.MemDataAlignment = DMA_MDATAALIGN_BYTE;
    hdma_usart5_tx.Init.Mode = DMA_NORMAL;
    hdma_usart5_tx.Init.Priority = DMA_PRIORITY_LOW;

    HAL_DMA_Init(&hdma_usart5_tx);

    // Link DMA handle with USART5 handle
    __HAL_LINKDMA(&usart5, hdmatx, hdma_usart5_tx);

    // Enable DMA interrupts
    HAL_NVIC_SetPriority(DMA1_Channel2_3_IRQn, DMA1_Channel2_3_IRQ_PREEMPT_PRIORITY, DMA1_Channel2_3_IRQ_SUB_PRIORITY);
    HAL_NVIC_EnableIRQ(DMA1_Channel2_3_IRQn);

    // Configure USART5 peripheral
    usart5.Instance = USART5;
    usart5.Init.BaudRate = SERIAL_UART_BAUD_RATE;
    usart5.Init.WordLength = UART_WORDLENGTH_8B;
    usart5.Init.StopBits = UART_STOPBITS_1;
    usart5.Init.Parity = UART_PARITY_NONE;
    usart5.Init.Mode = UART_MODE_TX_RX;
    usart5.Init.HwFlowCtl = UART_HWCONTROL_NONE;
    usart5.Init.OverSampling = UART_OVERSAMPLING_16;
    usart5.Init.OneBitSampling = UART_ONE_BIT_SAMPLE_DISABLE;
    usart5.AdvancedInit.AdvFeatureInit = UART_ADVFEATURE_NO_INIT;

    if (HAL_UART_Init(&usart5) != HAL_OK)
    {
        while(1);   // Need to switch out while(1) for proper error handling
    }

    // Enable UART interrupts
    HAL_NVIC_SetPriority(USART3_4_5_6_IRQn, USART3_4_5_6_IRQ_PREEMPT_PRIORITY, USART3_4_5_6_IRQ_SUB_PRIORITY);
    HAL_NVIC_EnableIRQ(USART3_4_5_6_IRQn);
```

Here is the `#define` that fails: 
```#define IS_DMA_ALL_REQUEST(REQUEST)             ((REQUEST) <= DMA_MAX_REQUEST)```

where: `DMA_MAX_REQUEST` is defined as:

```#define DMA_MAX_REQUEST              LL_DMAMUX_MAX_REQ```

and 

In stm32g0xx_ll_dmamux.h
```
#elif defined(STM32G0B0xx)
#define LL_DMAMUX_MAX_REQ                 LL_DMAMUX_REQ_USART4_TX
```

**Additional context**
Since there are the peripherals USART5 and USART6 that can use DMA on this platform, the proper define should be:

```#define LL_DMAMUX_MAX_REQ                 LL_DMAMUX_REQ_USART6_TX```

**Screenshots**

<img width="1920" height="1032" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/76bc10f0-b5ef-491b-b37e-6ff2de1c0e73" />

Hi

Why is the type of AHBPrescTable[] and APBPrescTable[] uint32_t?

  const uint32_t AHBPrescTable[16U] = {0UL, 0UL, 0UL, 0UL, 0UL, 0UL, 0UL, 0UL, 1UL, 2UL, 3UL, 4UL, 6UL, 7UL, 8UL, 9UL};
  const uint32_t APBPrescTable[8U] =  {0UL, 0UL, 0UL, 0UL, 1UL, 2UL, 3UL, 4UL};

All other families has got uint8_t

  const uint8_t AHBPrescTable[16U] = {0U, 0U, 0U, 0U, 0U, 0U, 0U, 0U, 1U, 2U, 3U, 4U, 6U, 7U, 8U, 9U};
  const uint8_t APBPrescTable[8U] =  {0U, 0U, 0U, 0U, 1U, 2U, 3U, 4U};

can you explain?

When updating a project to the latest HAL I was getting a hard fault when trying to TX a CAN message with my exisiting code base.

The differences exisit between 1.6.1 and 1.6.2.  A diff between the files confirms.

Reviewing the call stack the program was failing at this command:
/* Write Tx payload to the message RAM */
  for (ByteCounter = 0; ByteCounter < DLCtoBytes[pTxHeader->DataLength]; ByteCounter += 4U)

This was failing because the DataLength was out of range.

I went back to a saved version of my code to compare that area of code.  In the previous version this is what appears:
/* Write Tx payload to the message RAM */
  for (ByteCounter = 0; ByteCounter < DLCtoBytes[pTxHeader->DataLength >> 16U]; ByteCounter += 4U)

It appears between the two versions the DLC was changed from the shifted value to the absolute value.  This should be documented as it will eventually impact all of the DLC areas of my code.

